<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Build-host tool: bakes a handful of glyphs into rectangle runs the toolbar draws with plain
         FillRect, so an icon needs NO font at runtime. Three things that buys, each of which the
         runtime font path actually cost. It works where no emoji face is installed (any Linux host
         resolved none, so every emoji mark silently drew nothing). It is monochrome, so a mark tints
         and DIMS with the label beside it, which a COLRv1 glyph carrying its own palette cannot. And
         it renders identically on every machine, where the runtime path varied with whichever face
         the host happened to resolve.

         Run when the manifest changes, then commit the generated file. Never referenced by the app.
         Mirrors tools/BakeShaders, including living outside src/'s central package management.

         LICENSING: the source font must permit redistributing glyph shapes, because that is what
         baking does. Noto is OFL. A proprietary system face is not a valid source, and the runtime
         probe legitimately falling back to one does not make its outlines ours to commit. -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The rasteriser the renderer already uses for text, so a baked mark is the same shape the
         runtime draw produced, with no second glyph pipeline to disagree with the first. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\DIR.Lib\src\DIR.Lib\DIR.Lib.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
